  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1033 PM MDT Mon Oct 12 2026\n\nWe will have a nice couple of days, but it is looking more and\nmore likely that we will see a big changes to our weather by the \nend of the week. Currently our region remains sandwiched between \nan upper level trough over the Great Basin and an upper level \nridge over the Gulf coast. Many times a pattern like this would \ndraw up moisture, but for this time we are moving in drier air. \nSome of the moisture associated with what is left of Hurricane \nSimon, will be drawn up to our east (though just to our east). \nMost of the area will stay dry on Tuesday and Wednesday, with the \none exception being Hudspeth county which will have a slight \nchance for rain both afternoons, as it is closer to some of that \ntropical moisture. Temperatures both Tuesday and Wednesday will \nrun a few degrees above average east of the Rio Grande and a few \ndegrees below averages west of river. \n\nOn Thursday our weather starts to change. The upper level trough\nthat was to our northwest, will push east, but a chunk of energy\nwill carve and form a new upper level trough which will retrograde\nback to our southwest. At the surface a strong back door cold \nfront will approach the region from the east. This cold front will\nbring strong surface winds and dramatically colder air. High \ntemperature on Thursday will be similar to Tuesday and Wednesday, \nbut high temperatures on Friday will be 10 to 15 degrees cooler. \nAs the cold front pushes west Thursday night we will see a band \nof strong winds push across the area. Wind gusts in excess to 50 \nmph will be possible, especially for locations just to the west of\narea mountains. The strong gusty winds will slowly come down on \nFriday, but most of Friday will be cool with those brisk east \nwinds. The upper level trough to our southwest will try and lift \nsome of the moisture up over the cold front which will give lots \nof clouds and could give us a some precipitation both Friday and\nSaturday. The extended deterministic GFS keeps most of the rain \njust south of the area, but the extended deterministic EC brings \na good area of rain and maybe a mix of rain and snow (yes I said \nsnow) to the highest elevations of the Sacramento Mountains. If \nthe EC model is right, Friday will be a wet, cold and dreary day, \nif the GFS is correct is will be cool and brisk. Either way it \nwill certainly finally feel like Fall.",
